  "name": "Cartola",
  "editorial_article": "Born Angenor de Oliveira in 1908 in Rio de Janeiro, Cartola earned his professional name as a bricklayer, wearing a bowler hat to protect his hair from cement. A fundamental architect of the Estação Primeira de Mangueira samba school founded in 1928, he emerged as a central figure in Brazilian music despite frequent career interruptions. His work is characterized by non-trivial musical choices, including the use of tritone substitutions and modulations like I to bVI in songs such as 'Aconteceu'. Throughout the 1960s, his Zicartola restaurant served as a vital cultural intersection, bridging traditional sambistas like Pixinguinha and Nélson Cavaquinho with the emerging Bossa Nova scene. Though he recorded solo albums only late in his life beginning in 1974, his compositions, including 'O Mundo é Um Moinho' and 'As Rosas não Falam', remain cornerstones of the samba repertoire.",
